`)[1],"Make it look like the real thing, not like blocks: give each part the shape it is really made with. hull: a body you would draw in side, top and front views (car, truck and van bodies, cabs, appliance and device cases, seats, dashboards, castings and housings); the windows, doors, hood, lids, lights, grilles and bumpers on it are panel parts. loft: a form made by its cross-sections along its length (fuselages, boat hulls, wings, fan and propeller blades with twist, handles, oval bottles, organic forms). lathe with smooth: turned and spun parts (tyres, wheels, pulleys, spinners, nacelles, domes, nozzles). box with round: soft blocks (cushions, tanks, battery packs, pods). Keep plain boxes, cylinders, tubes and the other primitives for parts that really have those shapes: shafts, pipes, brackets, fasteners and small inner parts.","The parts appear one by one while you write the input, so write the large recognizable structure first (a car's body hull, then the panels on it and the wheels; an engine's nacelle and fan) and smaller or inner parts after, and write each part's fields in the order listed (shapes last). Then say in a sentence or two what the owner is looking at; the labels and part cards carry the details."].join(`

